n)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nProximity labeling of NF1 using APEX\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe goal of the study was to profile protein interaction neighborhood of NF1 by expressing NF1-APEX and profiling biotinylated proteins in proximity of NF1. Interactions were assessed at basal conditions or in the context of MEK inhibition with selumetinib.\n\n[METHODS]\nCells were incubated with 500uM biotin-phenol in complete medium for 30min at 37\u2009°C. APEX labeling was initiated by addition of H2O2 at a final concentration of 1mM and incubated for 30sec. To quench the reaction, H2O2 solution was discarded, and cells were immediately washed 3x with ice cold quenching solution (10uM Sodium ascorbate, 5mM Trolox, 10uM NaN3) for 1min x wash. Cells were scraped and collected in quencher solution, and pelleted. Excess solution was removed and cell pellets were snap frozen. To proceed with pulldown of biotin-labeled proteins, cell pellets were lysed in RIPA buffer (150mM NaCl, 50mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 0.5% sodium deoxycholate, 0.1% SDS, 1% Triton-X100) supplemented with 10uM Sodium ascorbate, 5mM Trolox, 10uM NaN3, 1mM DTT, and protease inhibitor (Complete protease inhibitor, Sigma #4693159001). Cell lysates were sonicated 3x at 15% for 7 seconds. Lysates were cleared at 13,000g for 10min at 4°C and protein concentrations were quantified using 600nM Pierce Assay Kit with Detergent Compatibility Reagent (ThermoFisher, #22662). Streptavidin magnetic beads (ThermoFisher, #88816) were washed 2x with RIPA buffer and 100uL washed beads were added per sample. Binding was allowed to proceed overnight at 4°C with rocking. Beads were collected on a magnetic rack and the following wash steps were performed at 4°C: 3x wash with RIPA buffer (5min each), 1x wash with 1M KCL (2min), 1x wash with Na2CO2 (2min),  and 2x with 50mM Tris-HCl [pH=8.0] (5min). Beads were resuspended in 200ul Digestion buffer (2M urea in 50mM Tris pH=8.0). Samples were reduced by adding 5mM TCEP and incubated at 37°C for 30min with orbital shaking at 1000rpm. Samples were alkylated with iodoacetamide at 5mM at room temperature, covered from light with orbital shaking at 1000rpm. The reaction was quenched with addition of 5mM DTT. Biotinylated proteins were digested with addition of 0.8ug mass spectrometry grade Trypsin (Promega, #V5111) overnight at 37°C with orbital shaking at 1000rpm. The following morning, an additional 0.4ug trypsin was added and incubated for 2hrs at 37°C with shaking. Samples were acidified with 0.5% TFA and desalted using C18 96-well plate format (Higgins Analytical, #96WR-20mg-W182).   APEX-labeled samples were acquired on a Bruker TimsTOFpro interfaced with an Ultimate3000 UHPLC. Peptides were resuspended in 0.1% FA and were separated using a 60 minutes linear gradient. Following loading, the percentage of B (80% ACN in 0.1% FA) was increased from 2% to 26% in 49\u2009min and then to 90% remaining time. The peptides were separated on a PepSep column (15\u2009cm, 150\u2009mm iid, 1.9\u2009μm beads size). The mass spectrometer was operated in positive mode using DIA-PASEF acquisition.41  The mass range was set 